What's new in amwall v2.0.2 (current)\par
\par
  - Programs listed with a Windows path shortcut now work. Entries\par
    written as %ProgramFiles%\\..., %SystemRoot%\\..., %AppData%\\...\par
    and similar -- which is how most entries in an imported\par
    simplewall profile are stored -- were shown in red as "does\par
    not exist" and, more seriously, never had their allow rule\par
    installed, so those programs were blocked with nothing on\par
    screen to explain why. amwall now resolves the shortcut to the\par
    real file everywhere it matters: the allow rule, the file\par
    check, the icon, the digital-signature and hash checks, and\par
    the live-connection highlight\par
  - Purge unused no longer deletes an imported profile. Because\par
    those same entries looked missing, Edit > Purge unused counted\par
    them as unused and would remove most of a freshly imported\par
    profile in one click\par
  - Your profile file is left exactly as it was. amwall resolves\par
    path shortcuts only when it needs the real file; it never\par
    rewrites them to fixed paths, so your profile stays portable\par
    between machines and user accounts\par
  - Column widths now fit their headings. Columns are sized to the\par
    heading text and the content instead of fixed widths tuned for\par
    English, so headings are no longer cut short -- most visible in\par
    translated versions, but it affected English too. Sizing is on\par
    by default and reapplies when you switch tabs, when a list\par
    refreshes, and after you resize the window\par
  - amwall now offers to restart with Administrator rights. It\par
    needs them to manage the firewall at all; previously it opened\par
    without them and quietly did nothing useful. You can still\par
    decline, and turning on Settings > "Skip User Account Control\par
    prompt warning" elevates silently from then on\par
  - New: amwall.exe -diagnostics writes a plain-text report of what\par
    amwall actually sees on your PC -- how every program path\par
    resolves, which would get an allow rule, whether any column\par
    heading is too wide, and what amwall has installed in Windows.\par
    It changes nothing and is saved to\par
    %APPDATA%\\amwall\\amwall-diagnostics.txt for bug reports\par
\par
What's new in amwall v2.0.1\par
\par
  - The main window now remembers its position and size. amwall\par
    saves where you placed and sized the window and restores it\par
    on the next launch instead of resetting each time. If the\par
    window was maximized it reopens maximized, and a window left\par
    on a monitor that is no longer connected is brought back onto\par
    the visible desktop\par
\par
What's new in amwall v2.0.0\par
\par
  - Real network meter. amwall now shows live download and\par
    upload speed -- per app on the Apps tab and per connection\par
    on the Connections tab -- along with a running total. It\par
    counts BOTH TCP and UDP, so a BitTorrent client or any\par
    UDP-heavy app shows its true rate, not just its TCP slice.\par
    Click a Speed column header to rank the busiest talkers\par
  - See which network each app is using. A new Interface column\par
    on the Apps tab names the adapter each app's traffic rides\par
    -- Wi-Fi, Ethernet, or a VPN tunnel -- so you can tell at a\par
    glance whether an app is going through your VPN\par
  - Connect prompts no longer stack up. If you step away from\par
    the PC without answering, amwall keeps a single prompt window\par
    per program instead of opening a new popup each time that\par
    program retries -- so you come back to one prompt per app,\par
    not dozens for the same few\par
  - Exe-swap protection is on by default. amwall verifies each\par
    allowed program by SHA-256 hash, so a program whose file is\par
    replaced on disk no longer silently inherits the old allow\par
    rule -- it is caught instead\par
  - Custom rules now accept IPv6 address ranges (start-end),\par
    matching the existing IPv4 range support and upstream\par
    simplewall\par
  - Firewall-correctness hardening. Default-deny now correctly\par
    blocks an app you've blocked even when it talks to a\par
    local/LAN address (the loopback allow no longer over-\par
    permits the whole private-address space). Importing or\par
    reloading a profile now re-applies the filters, so what the\par
    window shows is what the kernel enforces. A partially-\par
    corrupt or migrated profile no longer drops your entire app\par
    list -- only the bad entries are skipped. Early-boot\par
    protection now installs its block filters too, and loopback\par
    permits are hardened so another firewall can't override them\par
  - Reliability: a pre-release test gate now proves the\par
    default-deny firewall actually blocks outbound traffic on a\par
    live system before any build is published, so the\par
    non-enforcing regression seen in 1.1.17 / 1.1.18 cannot\par
    ship again\par
\par
What's new in amwall v1.1.19\par
\par
  - Critical fix: the default-deny rule could permit outbound\par
    connections instead of blocking them, so new apps were not\par
    actually blocked or shown for approval. Blocking now works as\par
    intended -- every unapproved app is blocked and prompts for\par
    allow / block. Anyone running 1.1.17 or 1.1.18 should update\par
    immediately\par
  - Connection prompts and the app list now also appear while\par
    another firewall or a VPN kill switch is active\par
\par
What's new in amwall v1.1.18\par
\par
  - Right-click menus everywhere. The Rules, Network, and Log tabs\par
    now have full right-click menus (edit / delete / enable /\par
    disable / copy / select all), matching the Apps tab and\par
    upstream simplewall. On the Network tab you can also close a\par
    live connection or jump to the owning app; on Network / Log you\par
    can create a rule pre-filled from the row\par
  - The app-list right-click menu is complete: assign an app to your\par
    rules from a "Rules" submenu, bulk enable / disable / remove a\par
    whole selection at once, and copy a row or a single column\par
  - Keyboard shortcuts for the lists: Enter (properties / edit),\par
    Delete, Ctrl+C (copy), Ctrl+E (open folder), Ctrl+A (select\par
    all), Ctrl+Tab / Ctrl+Shift+Tab (switch tabs), F11 (maximize).\par
    Typing in the search box keeps working normally\par
  - New Highlighting settings page. Turn each row-colour category\par
    (invalid / signed / system / has-connections / disabled-removal\par
    / etc.) on or off and pick its colour; double-click a row to\par
    change it. Fixes a wrong default colour for system apps\par
  - Fuller tray menu, matching upstream: Notifications and Packets-\par
    log submenus with their toggles, plus Website / About\par
  - Several fixes found in an internal review of the above: Delete\par
    no longer removes apps you marked "Prevent removal", and a\par
    keyboard Delete on the System / Blocklist rules tabs no longer\par
    acts on a stale selection from the Custom-rules tab\par
\par
What's new in amwall v1.1.17\par
\par
  - Firewall-correctness, security, and GUI release. Fixes\par
    30 issues found in a deep internal audit; the\par
    firewall-filter changes were verified against the live\par
    Windows Filtering Platform\par
  - DNS and DHCP could be blocked on a fresh install. System\par
    rules that name a Windows service (DNS/Dnscache, DHCP,\par
    and others) were silently dropped, so the always-on\par
    "block everything else" rule blocked core networking.\par
    Those rules now install correctly\par
  - Blocks now reliably beat allows, and the defaults match\par
    upstream simplewall. The "Block outbound/inbound"\par
    switches, a Windows-Update allowance, and the loopback\par
    list were ranked in ways Windows did not honour; stealth\par
    mode / IPv6 / block-by-default now start on like\par
    upstream. Upgrading keeps your existing protection\par
  - Localhost, private networks, and IPv6 work correctly\par
    again (the full loopback address set), and filters are\par
    now enforced from early boot instead of leaving a brief\par
    gap each restart\par
  - Emergency Reset no longer erases your app / rule lists\par
    when it can't actually turn the firewall off (for\par
    example when not run as administrator) -- it reports\par
    the failure instead\par
  - "Purge unused apps" no longer deletes protected or\par
    Windows-service / Store-app entries, and asks first\par
  - Safer when elevated: the log and debug files can no\par
    longer be redirected out of amwall's own folder via a\par
    folder-junction trick, and "Skip UAC" now reads the\par
    real system folders instead of ones a program could fake\par
  - Fixed memory / graphics-handle leaks (notification\par
    popups, the toolbar, the icon loader) that could make\par
    the window stop drawing over a long session\par
  - New in the app: Store (UWP) apps can be allowed /\par
    blocked from the app list; timed allows (15 min / 30\par
    min / 1 hr / 4 hr); the connect prompt re-asks after a\par
    while instead of once ever; the "Log to file" / "Log\par
    UI" toolbar buttons and the notification sound work;\par
    compressed simplewall profiles import for migration;\par
    per-app "Disable notifications" / "Prevent removal"\par
    toggles; and more keyboard shortcuts\par
\par
What's new in amwall v1.1.16\par
\par
  - Security hardening release. Fixes several issues found\par
    in an internal audit; the firewall-precedence fix was\par
    verified against the live Windows Filtering Platform\par
  - One bad rule no longer disables your whole firewall.\par
    A single malformed rule (or a rule pointing at an app\par
    you had uninstalled) could previously cancel the entire\par
    firewall setup at startup, leaving nothing blocked. The\par
    bad rule is now skipped and the default "block\par
    everything else" rule always stays in place\par
  - Blocks now reliably beat allows. Filter priorities were\par
    set in a way Windows did not rank as intended, so a\par
    blocklisted site could still be reached by an app you\par
    had allowed. Priorities now match upstream simplewall so\par
    a block always wins over an allow\par
  - Clicks land on the right row. In the Apps and Rules\par
    lists a checkbox, Edit, or Delete could act on the wrong\par
    entry when the list was sorted or filtered; it now\par
    always acts on the row you clicked\par
  - Running as administrator is safer. When elevated, amwall\par
    no longer launches a custom log-viewer program from an\par
    untrusted folder and keeps its log file inside its own\par
    folder\par
  - "Skip UAC" is safer. It will not turn on from a folder\par
    where another program could replace amwall.exe (for\par
    example, portable mode in Downloads); uninstalling now\par
    also removes that scheduled task\par
  - Hash checking now acts. With "verify by hash" on, a\par
    program whose file changes on disk is automatically\par
    disabled and its allow-rule removed (checked every 10\par
    minutes) instead of only showing a warning; "auto-allow\par
    Microsoft-signed" now also rejects revoked certificates\par
\par
What's new in amwall v1.1.15\par
\par
  - Filters now stay on across reboots and crashes by default.\par
    Two changes work together: (1) "Install boot-time filters"\par
    is now on out of the box, so the kernel keeps amwall's WFP\par
    filters loaded across a Windows reboot instead of wiping\par
    them at shutdown; (2) amwall remembers your last Enable /\par
    Disable choice in settings.txt and auto-reinstalls filters\par
    at launch when that choice was "on" but the kernel happens\par
    to be empty (fresh install, or an upgrade from a build\par
    that didn't ship boot-time filters on by default). Disable\par
    filters and Emergency reset both record an explicit "off"\par
    so an intentional shutdown stays shut down across restarts\par
  - Fresh installs now start with filters enabled on first\par
    launch -- no need to click Enable filters before the\par
    notifications mode begins prompting for new connections\par
\par
What's new in amwall v1.1.14\par
\par
  - Apps tab cold-launch row colours: actually fixed.\par
    The bug across v1.1.10-v1.1.13 was a message-id\par
    collision -- WM_USER_SIGNED_REFRESH (the worker's\par
    "I have new entries, please repaint" signal) shared\par
    its numeric value with WM_USER_CONNECT_BLOCK. The\par
    wnd_proc match-arm dispatch picked the connect_block\par
    arm first and silently dropped the signal because\par
    its wparam was null. The handler I kept editing was\par
    never being called -- four releases all changed\par
    code that didn't run. v1.1.14 moves the signal to\par
    a non-colliding id; rows now turn green as soon as\par
    their signature is verified, no resize needed\par
\par
What's new in amwall v1.1.13\par
\par
  - Apps tab Signed (green) row colours on cold launch:\par
    fourth and final attempt. Per a user observation that\par
    clicking Allow / Block on a connect prompt always\par
    produced the green colour for the affected row, copy\par
    that exact redraw sequence (a full populate of the\par
    apps tab via LVM_DELETEALLITEMS + per-row\par
    LVM_INSERTITEMW) on every batch the WinVerifyTrust\par
    worker finishes. Mutating the listview's item array\par
    is what makes comctl32 throw away its\par
    LVS_EX_DOUBLEBUFFER cache and re-query our colour\par
    handler -- earlier attempts (InvalidateRect,\par
    RedrawWindow, MoveWindow jiggle) all failed to\par
    invalidate the cached buffer. Rows now turn green as\par
    soon as their signature is verified, no manual resize\par
    or click required\par
\par
What's new in amwall v1.1.12\par
\par
  - Third try at the Apps tab cold-launch row colours --\par
    v1.1.11's RedrawWindow upgrade still wasn't enough\par
    because LVS_EX_DOUBLEBUFFER means comctl32 just\par
    re-blits its cached buffer on RedrawWindow; the cache\par
    only rebuilds (and therefore re-runs the colour\par
    handler) on a real WM_SIZE. The sign-verification\par
    worker callback now does the same MoveWindow +1px /-1px\par
    jiggle the post-resize cleanup uses, which DOES rebuild\par
    the double-buffer. Cold launch should finally show\par
    Signed (green) rows within a second or two of opening,\par
    no resize required\par
\par
What's new in amwall v1.1.11\par
\par
  - Fix Apps tab category row colours (Invalid / System /\par
    Signed / Pico) still not appearing on cold launch in\par
    v1.1.10. The end-of-create jiggle ran before the\par
    background WinVerifyTrust worker had populated the\par
    signed cache, so the first paint had nothing to colour.\par
    The worker's later "I have new entries, please repaint"\par
    notification was using a too-weak InvalidateRect that\par
    didn't pierce LVS_EX_DOUBLEBUFFER's cached surface --\par
    upgraded to a full RedrawWindow with RDW_UPDATENOW so\par
    rows turn green as soon as their signature is verified\par
\par
What's new in amwall v1.1.10\par
\par
  - Fix Apps tab not showing the category row colours\par
    (Invalid / System / Signed / Pico) on a cold launch.\par
    comctl32 painted the listview once during initial show\par
    before the signed-cache had settled and then never\par
    repainted; resizing the window fixed it because\par
    WM_SIZE already kicks the listview repaint pipeline.\par
    We now do the same kick as the last step of window\par
    creation, so cold launch matches post-resize visuals\par
\par
What's new in amwall v1.1.9\par
\par
  - Hotfix: v1.1.8 crashed on startup with a Rust runtime\par
    panic ("already borrowed: BorrowMutError") because the\par
    new rules-checkbox handler fired during listview\par
    creation (before any populate ran) and tried to grab a\par
    profile borrow that was already live up the call stack.\par
    The handler now uses try_borrow_mut and silently bails\par
    on any contention -- comctl32-synthesised notifications\par
    are skipped, real user clicks always succeed because\par
    they happen at idle\par
\par
What's new in amwall v1.1.8\par
\par
  - Enable / Disable filters is now near-instant. The install\par
    and uninstall paths now wrap their kernel calls in a\par
    single WFP transaction (FwpmTransactionBegin0 /\par
    FwpmTransactionCommit0) instead of issuing one\par
    synchronous-kernel-commit per filter. Full install with\par
    the bundled blocklist drops from many seconds to under\par
    a second, matching simplewall's responsiveness\par
  - New portable distribution: amwall-x86_64-portable.zip\par
    attached to every release. Unzip anywhere and run --\par
    profile, settings, and logs live next to the .exe\par
    instead of in %APPDATA%. Same self-contained binary as\par
    the MSI install, no system-wide footprint, no installer\par
    step. Useful for USB drives, sandbox VMs, secondary\par
    user accounts\par
\par
What's new in amwall v1.1.7\par
\par
  - Fix amwall.exe failing to launch with "VCRUNTIME140.dll\par
    was not found" on Windows machines that don't have the\par
    Visual C++ Redistributable installed. The C runtime is\par
    now statically linked, so amwall has no external CRT\par
    dependency and runs on a stock Windows install with no\par
    prereqs (binary grows by ~200 KB)\par
  - Fix the System Rules tab checkboxes being purely cosmetic\par
    -- clicking "Windows Update service" or any other system\par
    rule now actually toggles its effective enabled state,\par
    persists the choice across restarts, and re-applies the\par
    WFP filters immediately\par
  - Fix the User Rules tab appearing blank on a fresh install\par
    -- the 9 simplewall-style protocol presets (DNS protocol,\par
    FTP, HTTP, ICMPv4/6, IMAP/POP3/SMTP, QUIC, SSH, Telnet)\par
    now show up there alongside any user-added rules, all\par
    disabled by default and individually toggleable\par
\par
What's new in amwall v1.1.6\par
\par
  - Fix tray icon stuck grayscale + "filters off" tooltip\par
    after clicking Enable filters (self-heals via NIM_ADD\par
    fallback when the shell loses track of the icon)\par
  - Fix MSI installer aborting with "Error applying\par
    transforms" on English-default Windows (multilingual\par
    Template summary now lists the en-US base first)\par
  - New: hourly background update check. When a newer\par
